For 6 Larch Grove, the constraint check shows no conservation area, no listed building status, and no tree preservation order. That's good news, since those designations would normally bring extra controls.

On the general guidance, the council's outbuilding policy says a garden room usually doesn't need a planning application if it:

- is no more than 2.5 metres high,
- sits behind the front wall of the house, and
- covers less than half the garden.

If your plans meet all three, you probably won't need an application. If any of them is not met, you likely will.

I can't give a formal planning opinion, and I can't check your site plan directly. To confirm, please check the height and position of the garden room against the three points above. If it's close to any limit, or you want certainty, you can apply for a formal decision from the council (a lawful development certificate is one option), and the planning desk can advise on the right route.

If you already have a reference for any earlier application at the property, I can look it up for you.
